@* Table's Loading data page, moved from its Overview: rows from a database or an API, paging
   without a total, then a failed load. *@

<Heading Level="2">Data from a database</Heading>
<TextBlock>Pass an EF Core <Code>IQueryable</Code> as <Code>Items</Code>, and the database sorts, filters and pages. For more control, use <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> to load the rows yourself. It gets a <Code>DataRequest</Code> (the range as <Code>Skip</Code> and <Code>Take</Code>, the <Code>Sort</Code>, the <Code>Filter</Code>, and the columns, which say how each key reads an item) and returns a <Code>DataResult</Code>. <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> counts what the filter keeps and lists the page, through EF Core's async methods:</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Provider</Code>
<TextBlock>The filter becomes a LINQ expression on each column's own property, so the database filters, and a value typed into a filter never becomes code. <Code>request.Apply(query)</Code> does the same without counting; <Code>ApplyFiltering</Code> and <Code>ApplySorting</Code> do one part each. To filter a column your own way, such as a value stored differently from how it's shown, give it a <Code>Filter</Code> that returns the condition:</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@CustomCondition</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Data from an API</Heading>
<TextBlock>When the rows come from an API, pass the request on. <Code>request.Filter?.Find("status")</Code> reads one column's condition, and <Code>request.ToQueryString()</Code> writes the whole request (<Code>sort=-date&amp;skip=20&amp;take=10&amp;filter=total ge 100</Code>). The API reads it back with <Code>DataRequest&lt;Order&gt;.Parse</Code> and applies it through the fields it declares, so a request can't reach other properties. <Code>ToODataQuery()</Code> writes it for an OData endpoint, and the request also serializes with <Code>System.Text.Json</Code>. <Anchor Href="docs/common/data">Data and filters</Anchor> has the operators, the filter syntax and a full client and server.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock><Code>DataFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</Code> declares every public property, so read it from a type made for the API, or leave a property out with <Code>[ScaffoldColumn(false)]</Code>. The request asks for its own <Code>Take</Code>, so cap it on the server if it shouldn't ask for every row.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="csharp">@OverTheWire</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Paging without a total</Heading>
<TextBlock>When the data pages by cursor (an opaque "next" token, as many APIs and logs do) or has no total, set <Code>Paging</Code> and return the next cursor from your <Code>ItemsProvider</Code>. The table passes it back as <Code>request.Cursor</Code>; the total is optional.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Cursor</Code>
<TextBlock><Code>DataPaging.NextPrevious</Code> shows First, Previous and Next. Return <Code>Previous</Code> too if your source has it; without it, interactive tables go back through the cursors they've followed, and static ones offer First.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-cursor-pages" />

<Heading Level="2">Loading on scroll</Heading>
<TextBlock><Code>DataPaging.LoadOnScroll</Code> loads the next page as the last row comes into view and adds it under the rows: in place on interactive pages, and on static pages by following its Load more link, which enhanced navigation swaps in with the scroll kept. Give the table a <Code>MaxHeight</Code>, so it scrolls in its own box and what's under it stays within reach. Its Load more button, under the last row, is for the keyboard, out of sight until it has focus (<Code>LoadMoreText</Code> sets its text); with JavaScript off it shows, as a link that reloads the table with one more page of rows (<Code>?page=3</Code> shows the first three, asked of the provider as one request). With an <Code>ItemKey</Code>, a row already shown isn't added twice. A parent's re-render keeps the added pages; call <Code>ReloadAsync()</Code> to start over. Both modes also work with <Code>Items</Code>, where the table pages by row offset. (A visible Load more button is a list's pattern: see ListView.)</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-load-on-scroll" />

<Heading Level="2">When loading fails</Heading>
<TextBlock>If the <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> throws (or an <Code>IQueryable</Code> in <Code>Items</Code> does), the table logs the exception and shows "Couldn't load the rows." with Retry in place of the rows. On static pages and with JavaScript off, Retry is a link to the same address. <Code>ErrorContent</Code> replaces the message. When loading on scroll, the rows already shown stay, and the message and Retry go under them. A request the table canceled because a newer one replaced it isn't an error: pass <Code>request.CancellationToken</Code> on to stop the work.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-load-error" />

@code {
    private const string Provider = """
        <Table TItem="Order" ItemsProvider="LoadOrders" PageSize="10">...</Table>

        @code {
            async ValueTask<DataResult<Order>> LoadOrders(DataRequest<Order> request)
            {
                var query = db.Orders.AsNoTracking().Where(o => o.Active);
                return await request.ToResultAsync(query,             // filters, sorts, pages
                    count: (q, ct) => q.CountAsync(ct),
                    list: (q, ct) => q.ToListAsync(ct));
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string CustomCondition = """
        <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Tags" Filterable Filter="TagFilter" />

        @code {
            // Tags are stored lower case, as "red;green": "Green" in the filter finds it.
            static Expression<Func<Order, bool>>? TagFilter(FieldFilter f) =>
                f.Operator == FilterOperator.Contains && f.Value is string tag
                    ? o => (";" + o.Tags + ";").Contains(";" + tag.ToLower() + ";")
                    : null;
        }
        """;

    private const string OverTheWire = """
        // In the browser (WebAssembly): the provider calls the API with the request.
        async ValueTask<DataResult<Order>> LoadOrders(DataRequest<Order> request) =>
            (await http.GetFromJsonAsync<DataResult<Order>>(
                $"api/orders?{request.ToQueryString()}", request.CancellationToken))!;

        // On the server: only the fields declared here can be filtered or sorted.
        app.MapGet("/api/orders", async (HttpContext http, ShopDb db) =>
        {
            var request = DataRequest<Order>.Parse(http.Request.QueryString.Value ?? "")
                with { CancellationToken = http.RequestAborted };
            request = request with { Take = Math.Min(request.Take ?? 100, 100) };   // at most 100 rows
            return await request.ToResultAsync(db.Orders.AsNoTracking(), DataFields.Of<Order>(),
                count: (q, ct) => q.CountAsync(ct), list: (q, ct) => q.ToListAsync(ct));
        });
        """;

    private const string Cursor = """
        <Table TItem="AuditEvent" ItemsProvider="LoadEvents" PageSize="200" Paging="DataPaging.NextPrevious">...</Table>

        @code {
            async ValueTask<DataResult<AuditEvent>> LoadEvents(DataRequest<AuditEvent> request)
            {
                var page = await api.ListEventsAsync(cursor: request.Cursor, limit: request.Take ?? 200);
                return new(page.Items) { Next = page.Next };   // no total needed
            }
        }
        """;
}
